et a=[...e.querySelectorAll(`section[id], aside[id]`)],o=a.find(e=>`#`+e.id===location.hash&&e.getBoundingClientRect().top>=0&&e.getBoundingClientRect().top<innerHeight)??a.filter(e=>e.getBoundingClientRect().height&&e.getBoundingClientRect().top<=160).at(-1),s=n.getAttribute(`data-mode`);i&&(i.open=s===`work`),e.className=e.className.replace(/mode-\w+/g,``).trim()+` mode-${s}`,t.forEach(e=>{let t=e===n;e.classList.toggle(`active`,t),e.setAttribute(`aria-checked`,t?`true`:`false`),e.setAttribute(`tabindex`,t?`0`:`-1`)});let c=new URL(window.location.href);if(c.searchParams.set(`mode`,s),o){let e=a.indexOf(o),t=o.getBoundingClientRect().height?o:[...a.slice(0,e).reverse(),...a.slice(e+1)].find(e=>e.getBoundingClientRect().height);t&&(requestAnimationFrame(()=>t.scrollIntoView({block:`start`})),c.hash=t.id)}history.replaceState(null,``,c.toString()),document.dispatchEvent(new Event(`reading-mode-change`))})})});</script><div class="kpi-strip" data-layer="fact"><div class="kpi"><span class="kpi-value">+29,000<small></small></span><span class="kpi-label">payrolls in September</span></div><div class="kpi"><span class="kpi-value">4.2<small> %</small></span><span class="kpi-label">unemployment rate</span></div><div class="kpi"><span class="kpi-value">60,000<small> lower</small></span><span class="kpi-label">July and August, revised</span></div><div class="kpi"><span class="kpi-value">3.0<small> %</small></span><span class="kpi-label">hourly earnings, past year</span></div></div><section id="what-happened"><h2 data-layer="fact">What the release reported</h2><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="bls-empsit-2026-09"><p>On Friday, October 2, 2026, at 8:30 a.m. Eastern, the Bureau of Labor Statistics published the Employment Situation for September. Payroll employment rose by 29,000 and the unemployment rate was 4.2 percent. The bureau said both changed little. The unemployment rate has stayed between 4.1 percent and 4.3 percent since March, and 7.1 million people were unemployed.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="Both the unemployment rate, at 4.2 percent, and the number of unemployed people, at 7.1 million, changed little in September" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-3" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-schedule-2026-10-03/" data-passage="September 2026 | Oct. 02, 2026 | 08:30 AM" aria-label="Source 3: read the evidence">3</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="bls-empsit-2026-09"><p>July was revised down by 31,000, from a gain of 21,000 to a loss of 10,000. August was revised down by 29,000, from a gain of 162,000 to a gain of 133,000. The August release had reported a gain of 162,000. The two months together are 60,000 lower than previously reported.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="employment in July and August combined is 60,000 lower than previously reported" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-4" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-08/" data-passage="Total nonfarm payroll employment increased by 162,000 in August, and the unemployment rate was unchanged at 4.1 percent" aria-label="Source 4: read the evidence">4</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div><details class="receipt" data-layer="proof"><summary>July and August, as the September release states them</summary><figure class="data-table-wrap"><table class="data-table"><caption>Changes in thousands of jobs, from the September release.</caption><thead><tr><th class>Month</th><th class>Previously reported</th><th class>September release</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class>July</td><td class>+21,000</td><td class>-10,000</td></tr><tr><td class>August</td><td class>+162,000</td><td class>+133,000</td></tr><tr><td class>Two months combined</td><td class></td><td class>60,000 lower</td></tr></tbody></table></figure></details><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="bls-empsit-2026-09"><p>Employment in every major industry changed little. Health care added 17,000, slower than its average monthly gain of 33,000 over the prior 12 months. Ambulatory care added 13,000 and hospitals 12,000. Nursing and residential care lost 9,000. Construction added 11,000 and manufacturing 9,000. Financial activities lost 7,000 and are down 129,000 since a peak in May 2025.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="Health care employment continued its upward trend in September (+17,000)" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="Employment in all major industries changed little over the month&#10;nursing and residential care facilities lost jobs (-9,000)&#10;Employment in financial activities is down by 129,000 since a recent peak in May 2025">1</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="bls-empsit-2026-09"><p>The one-month diffusion index, the share of 250 private industries adding jobs, was 49.0 in September after 57.6 in August. The release says 50 percent is an equal balance between industries adding and cutting jobs. Private payrolls rose by 46,000 and government payrolls fell by 17,000. Information fell by 10,000. Temporary help services fell by 10.9 thousand.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="Total private (250 industries) | 50.4 | 54.4 | 57.6 | 49.0" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="50 percent indicates an equal balance between industries with increasing and decreasing employment&#10;Total private | 68 | 28 | 89 | 46&#10;Government | 8 | -38 | 44 | -17&#10;Information | -1 | 4 | -18 | -10&#10;Temporary help services | -11.7 | -10.0 | 0.2 | -10.9">1</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="bls-empsit-2026-09"><p>Average hourly earnings edged up 5 cents, or 0.1 percent, to $37.81. Over the year they are up 3.0 percent. The average workweek stayed at 34.4 hours. A change of about 122,000 jobs is what the bureau calls statistically significant in the employer survey, so the 29,000 gain does not clear that bar. The household survey's bar is about 650,000.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="average hourly earnings for all employees on private nonfarm payrolls edged up by 5 cents, or 0.1 percent, to $37.81" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="An over-the-month employment change of about 122,000 is statistically significant in the establishment survey">1</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="cnbc-september-jobs"><p>CNBC wrote that the average workweek was unchanged at 34.6 hours. That figure is mislabeled. The release says the workweek remained at 34.4 hours.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-11" data-record-href="/records/cnbc-september-jobs/" data-passage="The average work week was unchanged at 34.6 hours" aria-label="Source 11: read the evidence">11</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="remained at 34.4 hours in September" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-checked">checked</span></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="bls-empsit-2026-09"><p>The unemployment rate for Black workers rose to 7.0 percent. The bureau said that rate increased, and that the rates for adult men (3.9 percent), adult women (3.6 percent), teenagers (14.5 percent), and White (3.6), Asian (2.9), and Hispanic (4.7) workers showed little change. The labor force participation rate was 61.8 percent and the employment-population ratio was 59.2 percent. The labor force rose by 485,000 and household employment by 406,000.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="the unemployment rate for people who are Black (7.0 percent) increased in September" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="Both the labor force participation rate, at 61.8 percent, and the employment-population ratio, at 59.2 percent, changed little in September">1</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="bls-empsit-2026-09"><p>The long-term unemployed, out of work 27 weeks or more, were essentially unchanged at 1.9 million and were 27.1 percent of unemployed people. People working part time because they could not find full-time work were little changed at 4.5 million. People marginally attached to the labor force fell by 236,000 to 1.5 million. Discouraged workers were little changed at 414,000.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="The number of long-term unemployed (those jobless for 27 weeks or more) was essentially unchanged at 1.9 million in September" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="The number of people employed part time for economic reasons changed little at 4.5 million in September">1</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="bls-empsit-2026-09"><p>The broader underemployment rate, U-6, was 7.6 percent in September, after 7.7 percent in August.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="U-6 Total unemployed, plus all people marginally attached to the labor force, plus total employed part time for economic reasons, as a percent of the civilian labor force plus all people marginally attached to the labor force | 7.7 | 7.8 | 7.3 | 8.1 | 8.1 | 7.9 | 7.9 | 7.7 | 7.6" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-7" data-record-href="/records/fred-u6-2026-10-03/" data-passage="2026-09-01,7.6" aria-label="Source 7: read the evidence">7</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="bls-empsit-t09-2026-09"><p>The household survey counted 8,986 people working more than one job in September, 5.5 percent of everyone employed, against 8,805 in August. The table counts those people in thousands.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-2" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-t09-2026-09/" data-passage="Total multiple jobholders | 8,794 | 8,505 | 9,000 | 8,810 | 8,428 | 8,554 | 8,693 | 8,805 | 8,986" aria-label="Source 2: read the evidence">2</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="fred-prime-age-2026-10-03"><p>Prime-age participation, the share of people age 25 to 54 who are working or looking for work, was 83.7 percent in September, up from 83.4 percent in August.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-8" data-record-href="/records/fred-prime-age-2026-10-03/" data-passage="2026-09-01,83.7" aria-label="Source 8: read the evidence">8</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-official">official data</span></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="fred-ahe-2026-10-03"><p>On the hourly-earnings series, September's $37.81 is 3.02 percent above September 2025. The release rounds that change to 3.0 percent.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-9" data-record-href="/records/fred-ahe-2026-10-03/" data-passage="2026-09-01,37.81" aria-label="Source 9: read the evidence">9</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="Over the past 12 months, average hourly earnings have increased by 3.0 percent" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-official">official data</span></div></section><section id="where-this-sits"><h2 data-layer="fact">Where the month sits</h2><figure class="payroll-chart" data-layer="fact" id="payroll-chart"><figcaption><strong>Jobs gained and lost each month</strong><span>October 2025 to September 2026 · Seasonally adjusted</span></figcaption><svg viewBox="0 0 720 240" role="img" aria-label="Monthly payroll change. September 2026: +29,000 jobs. October 2025 to September 2026. Positive bars show gains; negative bars show losses."><g><line x1="48" x2="708" y1="169.33333333333331" y2="169.33333333333331" class="payroll-grid"></line><text x="40" y="173.33333333333331" text-anchor="end">-100k</text></g><g><line x1="48" x2="708" y1="122.66666666666667" y2="122.66666666666667" class="payroll-grid payroll-zero"></line><text x="40" y="126.66666666666667" text-anchor="end">0k</text></g><g><line x1="48" x2="708" y1="76" y2="76" class="payroll-grid"></line><text x="40" y="80" text-anchor="end">+100k</text></g><g><line x1="48" x2="708" y1="29.333333333333332" y2="29.333333333333332" class="payroll-grid"></line><text x="40" y="33.33333333333333" text-anchor="end">+200k</text></g><g><rect x="52" y="122.66666666666667" width="47" height="65.33333333333333" class="payroll-bar payroll-loss"><title>October 2025: -140,000 jobs</title></rect><text x="75.5" y="228" text-anchor="middle">Oct 25</text></g><g><rect x="107" y="103.53333333333333" width="47" height="19.13333333333334" class="payroll-bar"><title>November 2025: +41,000 jobs</title></rect></g><g><rect x="162" y="122.66666666666667" width="47" height="7.933333333333323" class="payroll-bar payroll-loss"><title>December 2025: -17,000 jobs</title></rect></g><g><rect x="217" y="48" width="47" height="74.66666666666667" class="payroll-bar"><title>January 2026: +160,000 jobs</title></rect></g><g><rect x="272" y="122