2026-09-01,159044" aria-label="Check September 2026: +29,000 jobs">+29,000</a></td></tr></tbody></table></details></figure><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="bls-empsit-2026-09"><p>September's 29,000 followed an average monthly gain of 45,000 over the prior 12 months, the figure the release states. Its three-month average line puts July through September at 51,000.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="following an average monthly gain of 45,000 over the prior 12 months" aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence" data-passages="Total nonfarm | 23 | 28 | 51 | 51">1</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="fred-payems-2026-10-03"><p>On the current payroll series, the twelve monthly changes through August average 45,250 jobs. 4 of the last twelve months were declines. The nine months of 2026 average 68,000 jobs. September's level is 159,044,000 and August's revised level is 159,015,000. The difference is 29,000, the same change the release reports.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-5" data-record-href="/records/fred-payems-2026-10-03/" data-passage="2026-09-01,159044" aria-label="Source 5: read the evidence" data-passages="2026-08-01,159015">5</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-computed">computed</span></div><details class="receipt" data-layer="proof"><summary>How those averages were counted</summary><p>Each month is that month's payroll total minus the month before, from the series saved October 3. The twelve months before September, September 2025 through August 2026, sum to 543 thousand and divide by 12 to 45,250. The release states that average as 45,000. The last twelve months include four declines: October 2025, December 2025, February 2026, and July 2026. The 2026 average is January through September, 612 thousand divided by 9.</p></details><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="cnbc-september-jobs"><p>CNBC reported that economists surveyed by Dow Jones had looked for job growth of 84,000 and an unemployment rate of 4.1 percent. Reuters, writing before the release, said its survey of economists expected 90,000 jobs and a 4.1 percent rate for a third month. CBS reported a FactSet forecast of 90,000. The Associated Press said economists had expected about 90,000.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-11" data-record-href="/records/cnbc-september-jobs/" data-passage="Economists surveyed by Dow Jones had been looking for job growth of 84,000 and an unemployment rate of 4.1%" aria-label="Source 11: read the evidence">11</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-13" data-record-href="/records/reuters-september-jobs-preview/" data-passage="Nonfarm payrolls likely increased by 90,000 last month" aria-label="Source 13: read the evidence">13</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-15" data-record-href="/records/cbs-september-jobs/" data-passage="Economists had forecast the economy would add 90,000 new jobs last month, according to financial data firm FactSet" aria-label="Source 15: read the evidence">15</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-16" data-record-href="/records/ap-september-jobs/" data-passage="Economists had expected September payrolls to come in around 90,000" aria-label="Source 16: read the evidence">16</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div></section><section id="chronology"><h2 data-layer="fact">Chronology</h2><div class="decision-timeline"><div class="dt-head"><span class="dt-date">Date</span><span class="dt-body">Decision</span></div><div class="dt-row"><span class="dt-date">September 4, 2026</span><span class="dt-body"><a href="/events/jobs/2026-09-04-august-payrolls-rise-162000/">August report: payrolls up 162,000, unemployment rate 4.1 percent</a></span></div><div class="dt-row"><span class="dt-date">October 1, 2026</span><span class="dt-body">Before the release, a Dow Jones survey looked for 84,000 jobs and a 4.1 percent rate</span></div><div class="dt-row"><span class="dt-date">October 2, 2026</span><span class="dt-body">Before 8:30 a.m. Eastern, Reuters&#39; survey looked for 90,000 jobs and a 4.1 percent rate</span></div><div class="dt-row dt-current"><span class="dt-date">October 2, 2026, 8:30 a.m. Eastern</span><span class="dt-body">The bureau publishes the September report: payrolls up 29,000, unemployment rate 4.2 percent</span></div><div class="dt-row"><span class="dt-date">November 6, 2026, 8:30 a.m. Eastern</span><span class="dt-body">Next Employment Situation, for October</span></div></div><div class="sourced-block detail" data-layer="fact" data-source="bls-empsit-2026-09"><p>The Employment Situation for October is scheduled for Friday, November 6, 2026, at 8:30 a.m. Eastern. The bureau's release calendar lists the same morning.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-1" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/" data-passage="The Employment Situation for October 2026 is scheduled to be published on Friday, November 6, 2026, at 8:30 a.m. (ET)." aria-label="Source 1: read the evidence">1</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-3" data-record-href="/records/bls-empsit-schedule-2026-10-03/" data-passage="October 2026 | Nov. 06, 2026 | 08:30 AM" aria-label="Source 3: read the evidence">3</a></sup></p><span class="chip chip-record">record</span></div></section><section id="records"><h2 data-layer="fact">The records</h2><p data-layer="narrative" data-absence>The Wall Street Journal and The Washington Post served only a subscription stub.</p><section id="records" data-layer="fact"><p class="sec-kicker">Sources</p><h2>The records</h2><p class="section-lede">Everything this page rests on, numbered where the story cites it. We keep a dated copy of each one.</p><h3 class="src-group">Primary documents</h3><ol class="src-list" start="1"><li id="src-1" value="1"><span class="src-n">1</span><a href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-09/">Employment Situation, September 2026</a> · released October 2, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the release&#39;s own payroll, unemployment, revision, industry, hours, and earnings figures</span></li><li id="src-2" value="2"><span class="src-n">2</span><a href="/records/bls-empsit-t09-2026-09/">Table A-9, multiple jobholders</a> · released October 2, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the count of people working more than one job</span></li><li id="src-3" value="3"><span class="src-n">3</span><a href="/records/bls-empsit-schedule-2026-10-03/">Employment Situation release calendar</a> · read October 3, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the publication time and the next report</span></li><li id="src-4" value="4"><span class="src-n">4</span><a href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-08/">Employment Situation, August 2026</a> · released September 4, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the August gain this release revises</span></li></ol><h3 class="src-group">Official data</h3><ol class="src-list" start="5"><li id="src-5" value="5"><span class="src-n">5</span><a href="/records/fred-payems-2026-10-03/">Nonfarm payrolls, monthly series</a> · saved October 3, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the levels behind the chart and the computed averages</span></li><li id="src-6" value="6"><span class="src-n">6</span><a href="/records/fred-unrate-2026-10-03/">Unemployment rate, monthly series</a> · saved October 3, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the rate series through September</span></li><li id="src-7" value="7"><span class="src-n">7</span><a href="/records/fred-u6-2026-10-03/">U-6 rate, monthly series</a> · saved October 3, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the broad underemployment rate</span></li><li id="src-8" value="8"><span class="src-n">8</span><a href="/records/fred-prime-age-2026-10-03/">Prime-age participation, monthly series</a> · saved October 3, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; participation for ages 25 to 54</span></li><li id="src-9" value="9"><span class="src-n">9</span><a href="/records/fred-ahe-2026-10-03/">Average hourly earnings, monthly series</a> · saved October 3, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the wage level and the computed year-over-year change</span></li><li id="src-10" value="10"><span class="src-n">10</span><a href="/records/alfred-payems-2026-10-02/">Payrolls as of the release day</a> · vintage October 2, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the release-day payroll level, which matches the series saved the next morning</span></li></ol><h3 class="src-group">Coverage checked</h3><ol class="src-list" start="11"><li id="src-11" value="11"><span class="src-n">11</span><a href="/records/cnbc-september-jobs/">CNBC</a> · October 2, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the Dow Jones survey of 84,000, and a workweek figure the release does not support</span></li><li id="src-12" value="12"><span class="src-n">12</span><a href="/records/cnbc-september-jobs-preview/">CNBC preview</a> · October 1, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the same Dow Jones survey, published the day before</span></li><li id="src-13" value="13"><span class="src-n">13</span><a href="/records/reuters-september-jobs-preview/">Reuters</a> · October 2, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; a survey of 90,000 jobs, published before the release</span></li><li id="src-14" value="14"><span class="src-n">14</span><a href="/records/abc-september-jobs-preview/">ABC News</a> · October 2, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; a preview expecting 84,000 jobs</span></li><li id="src-15" value="15"><span class="src-n">15</span><a href="/records/cbs-september-jobs/">CBS News</a> · October 2, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; a FactSet forecast of 90,000</span></li><li id="src-16" value="16"><span class="src-n">16</span><a href="/records/ap-september-jobs/">Associated Press</a> · October 2, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the post-release dispatch, including an expectation of about 90,000</span></li><li id="src-17" value="17"><span class="src-n">17</span><a href="/records/pbs-september-jobs/">PBS NewsHour</a> · October 2, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; the same Associated Press dispatch, carried by PBS</span></li><li id="src-18" value="18"><span class="src-n">18</span><a href="/records/usatoday-september-jobs/">USA Today</a> · October 2, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; USA Today&#39;s account of the 29,000 gain</span></li><li id="src-19" value="19"><span class="src-n">19</span><a href="/records/npr-september-jobs/">NPR</a> · October 2, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; NPR&#39;s account of the 29,000 gain</span></li><li id="src-20" value="20"><span class="src-n">20</span><a href="/records/upi-september-jobs/">UPI</a> · October 2, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; UPI&#39;s account of the 4.2 percent rate</span></li><li id="src-21" value="21"><span class="src-n">21</span><a href="/records/thehill-september-jobs/">The Hill</a> · October 2, 2026 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; The Hill&#39;s account of the 4.2 percent rate</span></li></ol><p class="sources-line"><a href="/methodology">How we check</a><span data-layer="proof"> &middot; where this page says <em>computed</em>, the arithmetic was done from the saved data series; where it says <em>record</em> or <em>checked</em>, the words trace to a saved document</span>.</p></section></section><section data-layer="fact"><h2>Since the previous story</h2><p><a href="/events/jobs/2026-09-04-august-payrolls-rise-162000/">Read the previous story</a></p></section><section class="revision-timeline" data-layer="fact" data-revisio